YEAR-ALIGNED EQUIVALENCE — the required before/after comparison.
An identical probe (TestEquivDump) was planted in BOTH copies (f479de3
and ec42346). It feeds the SAME legacy-year JSON document (2 income
sources, 2 expense sources + 1 removed, 2 one-time at years 1 and 5, 2
big-ticket at years 3 and 7 + 1 removed, 20-year horizon, fixed
start_date 2026-04) and dumps the FULL RunFast result (Projection,
PresentValue, BudgetFit, RMD, Tax, Sustainability,
ProjectionExplainability) as indented JSON.

  $ python3 difflib -u old.json new.json   (n=0)
  total diff lines: 5
  --- old
  +++ new
  @@ -30 +30 @@
  -    "note": "ends year 8"
  +    "note": "ends Apr 2034"

286 KB of engine output is byte-identical except the ONE budget-fit note
wording criterion 7 mandates (start_date 2026-04 + 96 months = Apr 2034).
Every balance, withdrawal, tax, RMD, PV and explainability figure is
unchanged.

The fixture is NOT vacuous: moving o1 to month 13 and b2 to month 85
(off their year boundaries) in the same probe produces 3877 diff lines
against new.json, i.e. those entries genuinely drive the numbers.

Semantic note verified while reading stepper.go: moving
`st.BigTicketRothEarnings = …` out of the year-boundary block is safe —
stepper.go:406 resets it to 0 at the end of every StepMonth after
consuming it at line 397, so in non-boundary months the new code assigns
0 where the old code already had 0. The equivalence dump confirms it.

